ef 1069 "q-2540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055179 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055179 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\frac{x}{5}+2=\\frac{2}{3}$, then $x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055179 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b53aeb0c-2291-515b-8b32-fdcd601499c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055179 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $2$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+2 & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}+2-2 & =\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}+0 & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, multiplying both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & =-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{5}} & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055602 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055602 #edb/ref 1061 175799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "362ed182-60db-5089-b1e6-79bd2e6be30b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055603 #edb/ref 1061 175800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80f9fb5b-00db-570c-a6f4-98191654603b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055604 #edb/ref 1061 175804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14f3a665-daa8-52a3-ae20-e94b27c5c513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055605 #edb/ref 1061 175809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3130b1e1-9a50-5105-90e9-828c84b859a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055606 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055606 #edb/ref 1061 175810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "827a7c9f-0805-5567-b393-4e871523a84e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055607 #edb/ref 1061 175813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df8a8dfe-a623-5ae2-b654-42325ba5b869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056012 #edb/ref 1064 "To solve an equation like $2x+4 =10$, we need to apply both the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t2x+4{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4} &=10{\\color{red}\\:-\\:4}\\\n               2x+0&=6\\\n\t\t2x &=6\n\t}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2x}{\\color{red}2} &=\\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}2}\\\n                \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}2}} &= 3 \\\n\t\tx &=3\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo $x=3$ is our solution. Let's plug this back into the original equation, to be sure that it's correct.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+4 & =10 \\\\ 6+4 & =10 \\\\ 10 & =10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** When solving these types of equations, we always apply the addition principle first, and the multiplication principle second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056012 #edb/ref 1065 8055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f8f52cdf-9de7-5b27-addc-4bc35612fa15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056012 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056013 #edb/ref 1064 "Solving equations with both the addition and multiplication principles can be a lot of work. There are often many steps to write down. However, we don't have to write down all of the steps.\n\nFor example, when using the multiplication principle to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{2} = 5,\n$$\n\nthe set of full steps are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{y}{2} & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}y}{\\cancel{2}} & =10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we cancel out the $2$'s to indicate that they disappear, then we can skip writing down several of the steps.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =5 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056013 #edb/ref 1065 8056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f3b97f5f-3831-50b9-bb82-d4f1b7b6f8c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056013 #edb/ref 1067 "Cancellation Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485777 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485777 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485777 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06277756-fc24-5507-b919-b617ef820898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485834 #edb/ref 1080 "y=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485834 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485834 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28a7a6f3-5966-516c-8785-4c6839f475c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486002 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\frac{11}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486002 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486002 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "87e6b7af-ba77-5346-b1ea-81d516760b33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486010 #edb/ref 1080 "y=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486010 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759